const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What is the medical coding career scope and salary in India?',
    a: 'Medical coding in India runs from a Coding Trainee or Junior Medical Coder role (roughly Rs 2-4.5 LPA) through Certified Coder (Rs 4.5-7 LPA), Senior Coder or Coding Specialist (Rs 6-10 LPA), Coding Auditor or QA Analyst (Rs 8-14 LPA), Coding Team Lead or Manager (Rs 12-20 LPA), up to Coding Operations Manager or Director of HIM (Rs 20-30 LPA and above) for people who stay certified and build specialty depth over 12+ years. The scope is driven by the US healthcare outsourcing industry, which continues to move revenue-cycle and coding work to Indian BPOs and RCM companies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need MBBS or a medical degree to become a medical coder in India?',
    a: 'No. Medical coding does not require a clinical license or medical degree. Life sciences graduates (B.Sc/M.Sc in biotechnology, microbiology, biochemistry), pharmacy graduates, nursing graduates, and general graduates from any stream are all eligible for medical coding certification courses, provided they meet the minimum aggregate requirement most institutes set (commonly around 50%). A life-sciences or paramedical background helps because it already covers anatomy, physiology, and medical terminology, shortening the certification timeline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between CPC and CCS certification for medical coding?',
    a: 'CPC (Certified Professional Coder), offered by AAPC, focuses on outpatient and physician-based coding using CPT, HCPCS Level II, and ICD-10-CM code sets, and is the more common starting certification for new coders. CCS (Certified Coding Specialist), offered by AHIMA, focuses on inpatient and hospital-based coding with heavier use of ICD-10-PCS, and generally suits coders with some coding experience already. Most freshers in India start with CPC because it has a lower experience bar to attempt and is the certification most Indian healthcare BPOs and training institutes are built around.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does a fresher medical coder earn in India?',
    a: 'Fresher medical coders in India typically earn roughly Rs 2-4.5 LPA (about Rs 18,000-35,000 a month), with the exact figure depending heavily on whether the fresher already holds CPC or CCS certification before applying, the city, and whether the employer is a healthcare BPO, a captive RCM unit, or a hospital chain. CPC-certified freshers consistently earn 20-50% more than uncertified freshers doing similar entry-level coding work.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is medical coding a good career in India with AI on the rise?',
    a: 'Medical coding remains a genuine growth career in India because AI-assisted tools are speeding up routine, high-volume coding rather than replacing the need for trained coders. What still needs a human is judgment on ambiguous or complex charts, compliance accuracy, and specialty-level reasoning, which is pushing the industry toward what analysts describe as the "seniorization" of entry-level roles: freshers are increasingly expected to build specialty and audit-level skills faster than earlier cohorts did. Coders who plan an early move toward auditing, denial analysis, or specialty coding protect their income growth better than those who stay on routine volume coding alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can medical coders in India work remotely or work from home?',
    a: 'Yes, remote and work-from-home medical coding roles are common in India, particularly with healthcare BPOs and revenue-cycle-management (RCM) companies serving US hospitals and clinics, since the work is entirely computer-based and does not require physical patient contact. Certified coders with a track record of independent accuracy are the ones most likely to be offered remote or hybrid arrangements; most freshers still start on-site or hybrid to build supervised experience first.',
